<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dost Mohammad Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1793">1793</a>–<a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dost Mohammad Khan was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in 1826 and assumed the title of emir in 1834. Deposed by a British invasion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war">First Anglo-Afghan War</a> in 1839, he was restored in 1842. He later brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> under his rule in 1855 and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 1863 after a ten-month siege, dying there days later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan.jpg" alt="Dost Mohammad Khan" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Company artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dost_Mohammad_Khan_of_Afghanistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dost Mohammad Khan was a Pashtun of the Mohammadzai branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> tribe, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>. His father, Payandah Khan, chief of the Barakzai, was killed in 1800 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaman-shah-durrani">Zaman Shah Durrani</a>, and his mother belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>. He was the eleventh son and spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a>, Persian, Punjabi and Turkish.</p><p>His elder brother Fateh Khan helped install <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-durrani">Mahmud Shah Durrani</a> in 1800 and restore him in 1809, but Mahmud Shah had him assassinated in 1818. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> brothers then stripped Mahmud Shah of everything but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and divided his lands, and Dost Mohammad received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, to which he added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, the richest of the Afghan provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Early in his rule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> valley to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nowshera">Battle of Nowshera</a> in 1823. Ranjit Singh used the dethroned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> prince Shah Shujah Durrani against him, and in 1834 Dost Mohammad defeated Shah Shujah under the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>. In 1837 his son Akbar Khan defeated the Sikhs at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jamrud">Battle of Jamrud</a>, where the Sikh general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh-nalwa">Hari Singh Nalwa</a> was killed.</p><p>He welcomed the British envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-burnes">Alexander Burnes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in 1837, but Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> would not accept his advances, and when he renewed relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the British set troops in motion in 1838. Shah Shuja was restored in Kabul on 7 August 1839 and Dost Mohammad was exiled to Mussoorie in November 1840.</p><p>He was restored after Shah Shuja was murdered in April 1842, and, convinced that war with the British would damage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, he sought an alliance with them. He subdued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazarajat">Hazarajat</a> and Bamian in 1843 and put down a Kohistani rebellion in 1846. His son Mohammad Akram Khan invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> in 1849, and the province was annexed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 30 March 1855 Dost Mohammad signed an offensive and defensive alliance with the British, and in 1857 he joined them against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. He took no part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>. In 1862 the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> captured Farah, which gave him a pretext to attack. Herat fell on 27 May 1863 after a long siege, and he died on 9 June, after forty years in the politics of South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><p>His son Sher Ali Khan succeeded him, and he was buried at the Gazurgah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. He ruled for 36 years. He had united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> and Herat in one state, as few of his predecessors had done, and state revenue rose from about 500,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> at the start of his rule to 7 million by his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dost Mohammad Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the collapse of Sadozai power, <b>Dost Mohammad Khan</b> of the Barakzai clan took Kabul in 1826 and assumed the title emir in the mid-1830s, building on Barakzai control from 1823.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1859 <b>Dost Mohammad Khan's</b> son Muhammad Afzal Khan conquered Kunduz for the Emirate of Afghanistan, ending its independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/kataghan-khanate">Kataghan Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dost Mohammad Khan</b> took Herat in 1863 after a ten-month siege, dying there days later, and Herat joined the Emirate of Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-herat">Principality of Herat</a></li><li><blockquote>Kandahar went to a group of brothers known as the Kandahar Sardars, led by Purdil Khan and later Kohandil Khan, who were half-brothers of <b>Dost Mohammad Khan</b> of Kabul.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kandahar">Principality of Kandahar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.62,65.72&amp;year=1838" aria-label="Kandahar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Dost Mohammad Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5416 4851h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5416 4851h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.62,65.72&amp;year=1838">What was here in 1838? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-afzal-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mohammad-afzal-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mohammad Afzal Khan</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1811–1867</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
